# HttpRequest not found in php

The error `ErrorException [ Fatal Error ]: Class 'HttpRequest' not found` is a classic symptom of a mismatch between your PHP runtime and its extensions, but it rarely stems from simply dropping a DLL file into an `ext` folder. The code snippet you are trying to run relies on the old PECL extension `php_http`. While this library was once popular for sending raw HTTP requests, the underlying ecosystem has shifted dramatically in the last decade.

When you encounter this specific fatal error after adding `extension=php_http.dll` to your `php.ini`, the issue is almost certainly that the extension itself is deprecated and incompatible with modern PHP versions. The PECL package `http` was officially removed from the main repositories years ago because it lacked security updates, did not support TLS 1.2 or newer by default, and offered a very rigid interface compared to what modern web applications require.

Simply replacing the existing DLL file you mentioned does not guarantee success if that DLL was compiled for a different PHP version than the one currently running on your server. Binary extensions are binary-bound; a `.dll` file built for PHP 7.3 will crash silently or fail to load entirely when placed in an environment running PHP 8.0 or later. You need to verify the exact version of PHP you are using via `php -v` and ensure any extension matches that version precisely, though given the age of this library, matching versions often won't matter because the project is dead.

Instead of fighting with legacy extensions that require specific DLLs, modern PHP development relies on built-in functions or updated libraries. The most robust solution for sending POST requests today is using cURL, which is included in PHP by default and actively maintained. It handles SSL certificates, redirects, and complex headers out of the box without needing external dependencies.

Here is how you can rewrite your original snippet using `curl`:

```php
$url = 'https://example.com/api/endpoint';

$ch = curl_init();

// Set method to POST
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POST, true);

// Set headers if needed (e.g., Content-Type)
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, [
    'Content-Type: application/json'
]);

// Pass the URL directly
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, $url);

// Do not return the response, but rather execute it
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, true);

// Execute the request
$response = curl_exec($ch);

// Check for errors
if (curl_errno($ch)) {
    echo 'Error:' . curl_error($ch);
} else {
    // Process $response here
    echo $response;
}

curl_close($ch);
```

This approach eliminates the need for `php_http.dll` entirely. If you are working within a Laravel application, the framework provides its own high-level abstraction via the `Http` facade or the `Guzzle` client, which abstracts these low-level details even further.

There is a specific scenario where legacy HTTP requests are still relevant: migrating older codebases that heavily relied on the `php_http` extension before it was deprecated. In those cases, you might find community-maintained ports of the old library on GitHub that support newer PHP versions, but you should treat them with caution regarding security vulnerabilities.

If your goal is to solve this within a Laravel context specifically, you do not need to hunt for DLL files or configure `php.ini` manually. The framework handles the underlying cURL configuration automatically. You can achieve the same result using Laravel's HTTP client:

```php
$response = Http::post('https://example.com/api', [
    'key' => 'value',
    'other_param' => 123
]);

if ($response->successful()) {
    echo $response->body();
} else {
    echo $response->status();
}
```

This pattern is standard practice. Unless you have a specific requirement to use the raw `HttpRequest` class from a legacy library, sticking to cURL or Laravel's built-in HTTP client ensures your application remains secure and compatible with future PHP upgrades.
